<section id="experiment-ledger"><h2>Give an experiment permission to fail</h2><p>Andrej Karpathy's <em>autoresearch</em> gives an agent a narrow editing surface and a fixed training window. The human specifies the objective. The agent changes training code, runs one budgeted experiment and records the validation result. The program file and experiment ledger make an unsuccessful idea inspectable. <a href="sources.html#R01">[R01]</a></p>
<p>A contributor repeated the setup with four instruction variants on one Mac. One variant recorded a prediction before each run and explained the result afterward. It found a better configuration in that run. The arms used unequal numbers of experiments, and each arm ran only once. The study offers a hypothesis for a controlled comparison. <a href="sources.html#R02">[R02]</a></p>
<h3>Try the same decision without a GPU</h3><ol><li>Open the <a href="labs.html#benchmark">sensor benchmark</a>. Pick one new seed before viewing the errors.</li><li>Write a prediction for ordinary accuracy and shifted accuracy. State why the badge should behave differently.</li><li>Lock the split generator and score function. Change only the feature rule.</li><li>Save one row per attempt with the change, prediction, result, budget and accept or reject decision.</li><li>Inspect one wrongly classified row. Revise the next hypothesis only if this evidence distinguishes two explanations.</li></ol>
<pre><code>Before the run: Removing the badge will improve shifted accuracy but reduce ordinary accuracy.
Decision rule: Keep a change only if its held-out tradeoff meets the predeclared requirement.
After the run: Record both scores, the seed and one error row. Preserve unsuccessful attempts.
Stop: One changed factor, three seeds, and one untouched final split.</code></pre>
<p><strong>Boundary case.</strong> Repeatedly choosing whichever change wins on the same validation rows can overfit the evaluator. Hold back a final set that the agent cannot inspect. If compute is scarce, spend the next run on a discriminating comparison. Extra attempts at the same guess can make a lucky winner look reliable.</p></section>
